---

<Base
  title="List"
  description="List: semantic collection of rows (ul/ol) with real anatomy - leading, content, trailing - dividers, interactive rows and order."
>
  <h1>List</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A ready <strong>with UI</strong>, not a <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> with typography. Below is a real <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> —the reader hears a list of N items—, and above it provides the row anatomy: <strong>leading</strong> (icon, avatar, number), <strong>content</strong> (title and description) and <strong>trailing</strong> (meta, badge, action), with dividers. It is piecewise composable and functional: an acting row is a real <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, never a <code>onClick</code> in the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The row, one slot at a time</h2>
  <p>
    The six examples that follow are <strong>the same list</strong>, and each one adds exactly one piece over the previous one. They are read in order: what appears new in each step is the only thing that changed in the markup.
  </p>

  <h3>1 · Only the list</h3>
  <p>
    The floor: a real <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> with rows and dividers, without a single slot. There is already row height, inset and hairlines, and the reader hears <em>list of 5 items</em>. Many lists do not need anything more than this.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={plainHtml} react={plainReact} lang="html" label="1 · List only" note="ul + item">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={plainHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListPlainDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>2 · Title and description</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__content</code>, the flexing spine. The title and description stack, and it is the only part of the row that shrinks: so a long title cuts there and does not push the row wider than its container.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={titledHtml} react={titledReact} lang="html" label="2 · Title and description" note="+ content">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={titledHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListTitledDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>3 · Media at start</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__leading</code>: an icon, an avatar or a number. It is <strong>decorative</strong>, it does not name the row — it is the title that names it. An empty slot does not exist for the layout, so rows without an icon do not carry ghost padding.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={leadingHtml} react={leadingReact} lang="html" label="3 · Average at the beginning" note="+ leading (Icon)">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={leadingHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListLeadingDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>4 · Finish at the end</h3>
  <p>
    Enter <code>sk-list__trailing</code> and the row is complete. It is anchored at the end with <code>margin-inline-start: auto</code> and brings <code>tabular-nums</code>, so the figures from different rows are aligned in a column. Accepts text or a component, such as a Badge.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={trailingHtml} react={trailingReact} lang="html" label="4 · Goal at the end" note="+ trailing + Badge">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={trailingHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListTrailingDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>5 · Row acts</h3>
  <p>
    Same anatomy, now wrapped in a real <code>a[href]</code> with <code>sk-list__action sk-interactive</code>. Focus and keyboard are native, and hover, focus, and press come from the <a href="/en/state-layer">state layer</a>. The <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> becomes only the one with the divider.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={linksHtml} react={linksReact} lang="html" label="5 · The row acts" note="+ a[href] + state layer">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={linksHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListLinksDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h3>6 · All together</h3>
  <p>
    The roof of the staircase: interactive rows, two pieces in the trailing, density <code>compact</code> and one row disabled. Nothing new about the component, just the above combined.
  </p>
  <Showcase code={fullHtml} react={fullReact} lang="html" label="6 · All together" note="+ density + disabled row">
    <div class="sk-list-demo"><Fragment set:html={fullHtml} /></div>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <ListFullDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={styles} label="CSS" />

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Root: <code>&lt;ul class="sk-list"&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code>. Each row is a <code>&lt;li class="sk-list__item"&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Row anatomy: <code>sk-list__leading</code>, <code>sk-list__content</code> (with <code>sk-list__title</code> and <code>sk-list__description</code>) and <code>sk-list__trailing</code>. All optional; compose the ones you use.
    </li>
    <li>
      Instruction Sequences: Use <a href="/en/components/process-list">ProcessList</a>, which persists a <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> and has the markers, sockets, and contents of each step.
    </li>
    <li>
      Functional row: an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with
      <code>sk-list__action sk-interactive</code> inside the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>. It fills the row
      and inherits focus, keyboard behavior, and the state layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Persistent state: none. List does not model current selection or destination; for active navigation use <a href="/en/nav-list">Nav list</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Variants: <code>data-density="compact"</code> and <code>data-dividers="none"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Without initialization, behavior remains that of the native <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <StylingHooks
    groups={[
      {
        file: "components/list.css",
        probe: "sk-list",
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /* A list is sized by its host: no width, the stage is flex and each `<ul>` shrinks to the width
     of its longest row, so the six examples came out at different widths and step 1 ended up at
     77px. The demo lends it the width a real column would have; the code shows the markup without
     that width, which belongs to the consumer.
     `is:global`: the authored HTML lives inside the srcdoc and doesn't carry Astro's scope attribute. */
  .sk-list-demo {
    inline-size: min(100%, 34rem);
  }
</style>
